k Management Framework", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework", "publisher": "US National Institute of Standards and Technology"}, {"title": "ISO/IEC 42001:2023 Information technology: Artificial intelligence: Management system", "url": "https://www.iso.org/standard/81230.html", "publisher": "ISO"}, {"title": "NIST AI RMF vs ISO/IEC 42001 crosswalk", "url": "https://alexandracar.substack.com/p/nist-ai-rmf-vs-isoiec-42001-crosswalk", "publisher": "Substack commentary"}, {"title": "NIST AI RMF or ISO 42001?", "url": "https://blog.balancedsec.com/p/nist-ai-rmf-or-iso-42001", "publisher": "BalancedSec"}, {"title": "NIST AI Risk Management Framework to ISO/IEC 42001 crosswalk", "url": "https://blog.rsisecurity.com/nist-ai-risk-management-framework-iso-42001-crosswalk/", "publisher": "RSI Security"}]}-->
<p>The security questionnaire used to ask about SOC 2. Now it often has a section on AI: how do you identify AI risks, who approves new models, how do you monitor them? Two frameworks dominate the answers, and they are often presented as alternatives. They aren't.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>Two different kinds of thing</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th></th><th>NIST AI RMF</th><th>ISO/IEC 42001</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What it is</td><td>A voluntary framework for managing AI risk</td><td>A standard for an AI management system</td></tr>
<tr><td>Structure</td><td>Four functions: govern, map, measure, manage<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td><td>Management system clauses plus AI-specific controls<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Certification</td><td>None; you use it</td><td>Certifiable by accredited bodies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Strength</td><td>Practical risk thinking across the AI lifecycle</td><td>Repeatable governance with audit and continual improvement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical audience</td><td>Internal teams, US government and buyers</td><td>Enterprise customers and international markets wanting assurance</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A common way to put it: the NIST framework is something you use, while ISO/IEC 42001 is something you certify against<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Commentators note that ISO's management-system elements, such as internal audit, management review and continual improvement, have no direct equivalent in the NIST framework<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>They map onto each other</h2>
<p>NIST's AI Resource Center hosts a crosswalk between the AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001. Commentators point out that it was contributed by the community rather than written by NIST, and that inclusion doesn't imply endorsement<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Informally, the four NIST functions line up with ISO's leadership and policy, context and impact assessment, monitoring, and review and improvement activities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Treat such mappings as starting points rather than proof of coverage.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Use NIST to decide what to do about AI risk. Use ISO/IEC 42001 to prove you do it consistently. They answer different questions.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I'd sequence them</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start with an AI system register.</strong> Every framework, and every regulation, begins with knowing which AI systems you have, what they do and who owns them.</li>
<li><strong>Apply the NIST functions to your highest-risk systems first.</strong> Map context and impacts, measure with real evaluations, manage with controls and incident response.</li>
<li><strong>Build the evidence as you go</strong>: evaluation results, approval records, incident postmortems, training records. These are what auditors and customers ask for.</li>
<li><strong>Add the management system</strong> when customers or markets ask for certification: policy, roles, internal audit, management review.</li>
<li><strong>Map once to many.</strong> The same controls support the <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">EU AI Act</a>, <a href="/insights/soc2-for-ai-startups/">SOC 2</a> and Gulf regimes such as <a href="/insights/difc-regulation-10-ai-systems/">DIFC Regulation 10</a>.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Where engineering carries the load</h2>
<p>Frameworks fail when they live only in documents. The controls that hold up in an audit are the ones built into systems: a model gateway that enforces approved models and logs every call, evaluation pipelines that gate releases as in <a href="/insights/evaluating-ai-agents-reliability/">evaluating AI agents</a>, incident handling as in <a href="/insights/ai-incident-postmortems/">AI incident postmortems</a>, and role-based training as in <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-ai-literacy-article-4/">AI literacy</a>. Build those and the paperwork follows; write the paperwork alone and it drifts from reality within months.</p>
</section>

